.Dd October 26, 2023
.Dt MEM 4
.Os Substrate
.Sh NAME
.Nm mem ,
.Nm kmem
.Nd memory files
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In fcntl.h
.Ft int
.Fn open "/dev/mem" "flags"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm mem
file is a character device file that provides access to the physical memory of the computer.
Byte offsets in this file are interpreted as physical memory addresses.
Reading and writing to this file allows user-mode processes to access hardware devices mapped into physical memory (MMIO) or to inspect physical RAM.

The
.Nm kmem
file provides access to the virtual memory of the kernel.
Byte offsets in this file are interpreted as kernel virtual addresses.

.Sh SECURITY
Access to
.Nm mem
is restricted to the superuser (UID 0) and/or processes with the
.Dv CAP_SYS_RAWIO
capability.
In addition, the system
.Va securelevel
configuration may further restrict access:
.Bl -tag -width indent
.It Level 0
Read and write access permitted.
.It Level 1 or higher
Write access is denied. Read access may be restricted to read-only ranges.
.El

.Sh IOCTLS
The
.Nm mem
device supports standard character device ioctls.
Management ioctls for configuring allowed ranges may be supported in the future.

.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/kmem" -compact
.It Pa /dev/mem
Physical memory.
.It Pa /dev/kmem
Kernel virtual memory.
.El

.Sh ERRORS
.Bl -tag -width Er
.It Bq Er EPERM
Permission denied (non-root user or securelevel restriction).
.It Bq Er EFAULT
Bad address (user buffer invalid) or physical address fault.
.It Bq Er EIO
Bus error or hardware error while accessing the physical address.
.It Bq Er ENXIO
Address out of valid physical range (e.g. beyond max RAM and not MMIO).
.El

.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr securelevel 7 ,
.Xr mmap 2
